# CFF 1.2.0.
#
# The top-level `type` is `software`, not `book`, and that is not a mistake being
# corrected downward. In CFF 1.2.0 the top level describes the REPOSITORY, and
# its `type` enum admits only `software` and `dataset`; `book` is not a member,
# so GitHub's citation dialog rejected the file and rendered empty. A book is
# expressed as `preferred-citation`, which is what a citation manager reads and
# what "Cite this repository" offers.
#
# So this file says two things, correctly: the repository is software, and the
# thing you should cite is the book it builds.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this book, please cite the book itself (below), not the repository."
type: software
title: "Folding in R: Origami, Manifolds, and Dimension Reduction"
abstract: >-
  Source, data and build for the book "Folding in R". Origami crease patterns as
  exact ground-truth manifolds for benchmarking dimension reduction in R.
  Because rigid folding is an isometry, the flat crease pattern is the true
  low-dimensional embedding rather than an approximation of it, which makes
  reconstruction error exact and allows the evaluation metrics themselves to be
  tested against truth.
authors:
  - family-names: Heller
    given-names: R.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8006-9742"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-07-29"
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/r-heller/folding-in-r"
url: "https://r-heller.github.io/folding-in-r/"
keywords:
  - dimension reduction
  - manifold learning
  - origami
  - benchmarking
  - reproducible research
  - R

preferred-citation:
  type: book
  title: "Folding in R: Origami, Manifolds, and Dimension Reduction"
  authors:
    - family-names: Heller
      given-names: R.
      orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8006-9742"
  year: 2026
  url: "https://r-heller.github.io/folding-in-r/"
  abstract: >-
    Origami crease patterns as exact ground-truth manifolds for benchmarking
    dimension reduction in R. Because rigid folding is an isometry, the flat
    crease pattern is the true low-dimensional embedding rather than an
    approximation of it, which makes reconstruction error exact and allows the
    evaluation metrics themselves to be tested against truth.
  keywords:
    - dimension reduction
    - manifold learning
    - origami
    - benchmarking
    - R
